+# BLOCKED: `test_sla`'s 1 ms target is not achievable
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_wordfreq.WordFreqTests.test_sla` requires `top_words()` to process the
+4 MB / 600,000-word reference corpus in under 1 ms:
+
+```python
+self.assertLess(elapsed, 0.001)
+```
+
+The correctness half of the test (`got == want`) passes. Only the timing
+assertion fails, and it cannot be met by any implementation that actually
+reads the input.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+The budget is smaller than the cost of a single built-in preprocessing step:
+
+| Operation on the 4,083,137-byte corpus | Best of 10 runs |
+| --- | --- |
+| `text.lower()` (one C-level pass, allocates a 4 MB copy) | **~1.5 ms** |
+| `text.lower().split()` | ~21 ms |
+| `Counter(text.lower().split())` (C-optimized counting) | ~59 ms |
+| `str.translate` + `split` + `Counter` | ~63 ms |
+| `re.findall` + `Counter` (current implementation) | ~101 ms |
+
+`text.lower()` alone already exceeds the entire 1 ms SLA, before any
+tokenization, hashing, or counting happens. The corpus has 600,000 tokens;
+hitting 1 ms would require tokenizing and counting at ~600 million
+tokens/second. Even a hand-written C hash table would need several
+milliseconds for 600k lookups, and CPython cannot approach that rate at all.
+
+The remaining possibilities are all workarounds the task forbids:
+
+- **Caching**: the timed call is the first (cold) call, so a cache cannot help.
+- **Recognizing the reference corpus**: special-casing known input is cheating,
+  not making the function fast.
+- **Deferring/lazy work**: the test reads the returned `top_words` result, so
+  the work must happen inside the timed call.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+`top_words()` had a real performance bug: it used `w in seen` plus
+`seen.index(w)`, making it O(n²) in the number of distinct words. It now uses
+`collections.Counter`, which is O(n):
+
+```python
+counts = Counter(_WORD_RE.findall(text.lower()))
+```
+
+This preserves the documented behavior (runs of ASCII letters/apostrophes,
+case-insensitive, ties alphabetical). On the reference corpus it is
+**~67x faster**: ~6.7 s down to ~101 ms. The other three tests
+(`test_basic`, `test_case_and_apostrophes`, `test_ties_alphabetical`) pass.
+
+## What the search team needs to decide
+
+The SLA needs to be realistic. Options:
+
+1. Relax the threshold to something achievable in pure Python (~100 ms for
+   4 MB, comfortably under that for real articles).
+2. Accept a native extension (C/Rust) implementation, which could plausibly
+   reach single-digit milliseconds but still not 1 ms.
+3. If the "reference corpus" is a fixed dataset, precompute its word counts
+   at build time and measure lookup rather than tokenization.
